New York Yankees manager Yogi Berra has said how pleased he is with the way Omar Moreno is playing.
"He's having a hell of a spring," Berra said, "and I wanted to let him know it wasn't going unnoticed. His frame of mind has been exceptional."
Moreno, who has plenty of playing time because of injuries in the outfield, is hitting.351 and leads in runs scored, with 10.
There's just one problem: When the injured players, Rickey Henderson and Dave Winfield, return, Moreno will be a backup.
It's a role Berra wishes the 31-year-old Moreno would accept, but that's not likely.
"He could be extremely valuable to this team," Berra said, "because he gives us additional speed, good defense and the ability to produce runs. I understand his desire to play every day. I understand any player's desire to play every day."
Moreno didn't play daily in 1984. He played 117 games, got 355 at-bats and hit.259. He didn't like the part-time role.
In the offseason, the Yankees traded for Henderson. They have tried to trade Moreno and almost had a deal completed with a National League team, but it collapsed. Moving Moreno isn't easy because of a $600,000-a-year contract and unusual incentive clauses.
"It will be hard for me if I don't play every day," Moreno said. "I've played well this spring. They ask me to report in good shape and play hard. I have.
"I would like to go back to the National League. I'm healthy. I'm young. And I can still play."
It's important to Moreno that he isn't viewed as a troublemaker.
"I just want an opportunity to play every day, and they brought Rickey Henderson here to play center field.
"The Yankees are a good team to play for. They have great tradition, and everybody wants to see the Yankees play."
Moreno said, he prefers the National League's style of play.
"They play aggressive, fast-paced baseball in the National League," Moreno said. "You seldom see a 2 hour, 45-minute game.
"In the American League, it's more of a power game. The National League goes more for one run at a time, line-drive hitting and good pitching. The game is quicker."
But what happens if Moreno ends up as the odd-man-out in the Yankees' outfield?
"My agent, Tom Reich, knows how well I'm playing," Moreno said. "If it becomes necessary to talk to the management, he will do it, not me."
Berra hopes it doesn't become necessary.
"You never know when you'll have injuries," Berra said. "Look at what has happened to us this spring. There's a place for Moreno on this team, but I want him to be happy."{r}
