Allegations that a state caseworker lied in a report involving a murdered 7- year-old contradicted the findings Wednesday of an independent committee that cleared the caseworker of any wrongdoing.
Reed Hauff died of head injuries received in a beating April 25, just two days after state Department of Health and Rehabilitative Services counselor Orea Sanchez had been at the boy's house in response to a medical abuse complaint.
A Broward County grand jury Wednesday indicted the child's guardian, Jeffrey Lynn Hock, 36, of Wilton Manors on a first-degree murder charge in the beating of Hauff. He is being held without bond.
Hauff, described as an active but small youngster for his age, had been living with the Hock family since January, after the boy's father asked Jeffrey Hock to care for his son.
But Hock's wife, Linda, said while waiting to testify before the grand jury that the HRS counselor had lied in the report on the April 23 interview, which stated: "When asked if the boy was being abused by her husband, Mrs. Hock replied 'no.' "
Mrs. Hock, 31, said she had notified HRS that day that her husband was abusing the boy.
After receiving an anonymous telephone call, Sanchez and Wilton Manors Detective Rick Wiley interviewed members of the Hock family at about 4 p.m. at their home.
"She was a beaten and abused wife. And she reported the child abuse to HRS. Why, then, would she tell the counselor there was no abuse?" said Mrs. Hock's attorney, Bruce Lyons. "I'll tell you why... HRS knows it screwed up."
According to Detective Wiley's report on the April 23 interview, Mrs. Hock said her husband often would wake Reed in the middle of the night and spank him as punishment for things the boy had done during the day.
She also told the detective that Hock threatened to hold the boy's hands under scalding-hot water, his report says.
The detective reported that Mrs. Hock said her husband struck her and would throw her over chairs if she talked back to him.
"The HRS counselor was there during the interview and heard the same things I heard. I don't know why the report was written the way it was," Wiley said.
The handling of the case by HRS was reviewed by the Human Rights Advocacy Committee, a watchdog group that can recommend changes in the department's procedures.
"In reviewing this case from its inception, the committee could obtain no information that could have substantiated removing this child from his home on April 23, 1985. All the information that is now available, after the tragic death of this child, was not available to the HRS counselor and the police detective on that date," the committee said.
HRS District Administrator John Stokesberry could not be reached for comment on either Mrs. Hock's allegations or the committee's findings.
The comatose boy was taken to Holy Cross Hospital the morning of April 26 after being struck in the head the night before.
The hospital transferred Hauff to Miami Children's Hospital, where the boy lay in a coma for five days before life-support machines were turned off and he died.
