MIAMI - Oklahoma came close enough to smell, but not taste. Near enough to touch, but not grasp. Proximate enough to dream, but not wake to realized fantasy.
Instead, Urban Meyer did the tasting.
Percy Harvin did the grasping.
Tim Tebow realized the fantasy.
And Florida won its second BCS national championship in three years, its 24-14 victory over Oklahoma on Thursday night in Dolphin Stadium a triumph of Southeastern Conference substance over Big 12 Conference style.
The most fans (78,468) ever to witness a football game at Dolphin Stadium got a chance to see Meyer, Florida's coach, become the first coach to win two BCS titles. And to watch Harvin, the multitalented junior receiver, return from an ankle injury and look as sleek as ever while running for 122 yards and a touchdown on nine carries and catching five passes for 49 yards.
And to observe Tebow, the junior quarterback and 2007 Heisman Trophy winner, become the Most Outstanding Player of the game by producing a title-game classic that Florida fans will call vintage Tebow: 18 for 30 passing for 231 yards and two touchdowns - including a 4-yard, jump pass score with 3:07 left to provide the final points - with a couple of interceptions, and 22 carries for a rugged 109 yards.
"I think we learned we have to come out every week and play with passion," Tebow said.
Oklahoma learned what it's like to be on the other end of that passion, which came to a head following Florida's only loss, a 31-30 home defeat to Ole Miss, and spilled over every game thereafter.
The Gators (13-1) became the second team, behind LSU, to win two BCS championships by pouring sugar in the gas tank of Oklahoma's Ferrari offense. By the end the Sooners looked spiffy, but weren't moving at all. They might as well have been sitting on blocks.
Oklahoma (12-2) entered the game having scored more than 60 points in five consecutive games and having set a Division I record with 702 points.
But rather than a point-a-minute offense against Florida, the Sooners couldn't manage a point for every four minutes. Florida ran more plays (74-70), gained more yards (480-363), converted twice as many third downs (12 of 17 to 6 of 13) and held the ball almost 10 minutes longer (34:57-25:03).
All that, and a couple of forced turnovers, added up to a fifth consecutive loss in a BCS bowl by Oklahoma.
Sure, it might not end the championship debate; Utah, USC and Texas all already have staked their claim to the title in The Associated Press media poll. But the BCS title, awarded by the coaches, is a wrap. The marks all over it look like Gator prints.
"I think the turnovers were the difference," Meyer said. "We're going to enjoy the national championship, let somebody else worry about (the other polls and lobbying). Gators are No. 1."
Florida led 7-7 at halftime and, no, that's no misprint. Because if ever a team was entitled to believe it led a tie game, it was the Gators.
Tebow threw a touchdown in the first half, a 20-yarder to Louis Murphy with 14:02 left in the second quarter to give Florida a 7-0 lead. But he sandwiched it with two interceptions, the first two-interception game of his career and as many picks in 17 first-half pass attempts as he had thrown the entire season, in 268 attempts, entering the game.
Oklahoma punted after the first interception, then fared much worse after the second.
After driving to the Florida 1 on three plays from the 26, Sooners running back Chris Brown was stopped for no gain on third-and-goal. But rather than kick a field goal on fourth-and-goal from the 1 to take a 10-7 lead, the Sooners opted for a run to the left side.
Bad move.
Brown, a junior from Alexandria, was smacked for a 2-yard loss by Gators defensive tackle Torrey Davis with 5:20 left in the first half.
And to add one final indignity, on first-and-goal from the 6 with 10 seconds left in the half, Oklahoma quarterback Sam Bradford threw a pass to the goal line that seemingly was tipped in{g} the{r} end{r} zone{g}.{r}
{r}But{r} the{r} Soon{g}ers{g} couldn{g}'t{g} get{r} the{r} ball{g} back{g}.{g}
